{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013bYqxGyS1veCjHBNZohgXY","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Paper Framing — ec_hte_llm_annotation\n2\t\n3\t## Target Venue\n4\t- Primary: EMNLP 2026 Main (via ARR)\n5\t- Deadline: ARR submission May 25, 2026; commitment deadline August 2, 2026\n6\t- Format: Long paper, 8 pages + unlimited references\n7\t\n8\t## Core Claims (3 条，可证伪)\n9\t\n10\t1. **Bias Mechanism Discovery**: When LLM misclassification rates for an effect modifier vary across subgroups, applying a global (pooled) correction to subgroup-level CATE estimates introduces systematic bias — over-correcting low-error subgroups and under-correcting high-error ones. In realistic settings, this \"debiasing\" can worsen CATE bias by 2-3x (median across configurations), with extreme cases exceeding 20x. Global correction increases subgroup-level bias in ~50% of the parameter space.\n11\t\n12\t2. **EC-HTE Solution**: Hierarchical Bayesian subgroup-stratified correction (EC-HTE) substantially reduces subgroup-level bias while controlling estimation variance through partial pooling. HB provides meaningful RMSE reduction in moderate-sized subgroups by borrowing strength across groups (classic James-Stein shrinkage). **Boundary condition**: when expert labels per subgroup < ~25 (e.g., n_expert=50 with 12% minority subgroup → ~6 labels) AND subgroup parameter is far from group mean, shrinkage can be harmful — stratified MLE is preferred. Recommended: n_expert such that smallest subgroup contains ≥25 expert labels. **NOTE**: exact improvement magnitudes pending full 100 MC confirmation (current estimates from n_mc=2 dry-run). Demonstrated with both difference-in-means and CausalForestDML (exp-011: consistent bias reduction pattern, ec_hte_forest |bias|=0.06 vs naive 0.16). CausalForest amplifies the debiasing-hurts effect (up to 5.9x in K=4/extreme) due to nonlinear CATE estimation.\n13\t\n14\t3. **Diagnostic Criterion**: The product of misclassification heterogeneity × CATE heterogeneity across subgroups provides a computable diagnostic: when this product exceeds a threshold, global correction is harmful and stratified correction is needed. This criterion has standalone value independent of EC-HTE.\n15\t\n16\t## Story Spine\n17\t\n18\t- **Problem**: NLP researchers increasingly use LLM annotations as effect modifiers in heterogeneous treatment effect analysis across computational social science (CSS) — e.g., sentiment framing moderates message effectiveness, hate speech type moderates intervention impact. LLM misclassification is non-random — error rates vary by subgroup (Ashwin et al., 2025: error varies by refugee status, gender, education; documented across CSS tasks including sentiment, stance, toxicity). Current PPI/DSL corrections apply global rectifiers assuming homogeneous error.\n19\t\n20\t- **Existing approaches & gaps**: Mozer (2026) proves arm-specific rectifiers fix ATE bias under differential prediction error, but explicitly limits analysis to average treatment effects. PPI (Angelopoulos et al., 2023) and DSL (Egami et al., 2024) correct population-level estimates. PPCI (Sui et al., 2026) extends PPI to conditional functionals but corrects outcome-side measurement, not covariate-side. ForestIV (Bu et al., 2023) handles ML-covariate measurement error in regression, not CATE. **No work addresses correction when the mismeasured variable defines the heterogeneity structure.**\n21\t\n22\t- **Our framing**: We identify and formalize a previously unrecognized bias mechanism — measurement-error × causal-heterogeneity confounding. When a pooled confusion matrix is applied to correct subgroup-level CATEs, it misattributes prediction noise across subgroups with different error rates, producing bias proportional to the product of misclassification heterogeneity and CATE heterogeneity. This is a problem-level discovery, not a technique-level extension. EC-HTE solves it via hierarchical Bayesian stratified correction with honest causal forest integration. **Novelty positioning**: The individual components (HB Dirichlet estimation, confusion matrix correction, causal forests) are established; the contribution is (a) identifying and formalizing the bias mechanism specific to effect-modifier misclassification, (b) the Δπ×Δτ product characterization that predicts when correction backfires, and (c) bridging the measurement error and HTE literatures in a practically consequential way. Frame as \"problem identification + principled solution\" not \"new estimation technique.\"\n23\t\n24\t- **Headline result template**: \"On semi-synthetic CSS benchmarks (sentiment analysis, hate speech detection), global correction worsens subgroup CATE bias by 2-3x (median), with up to 20x in extreme cases; EC-HTE reduces bias to near-zero while maintaining 92-97% coverage, with as few as 250 expert labels.\"\n25\t\n26\t- **Oracle framing**: When reporting DGP C results where ec_hte_ppci outperforms oracle, always use \"covariate-oracle\" (true Z + observed Y), never unqualified \"oracle\". Frame as \"composite correction is necessary and sufficient when both error types coexist\" — NOT \"beats oracle\". The result is by construction (ec_hte_ppci corrects both error types; covariate-oracle only corrects one), not surprising.\n27\t\n28\t## Key Contributions (ordered for paper)\n29\t\n30\t1. **Bias decomposition identity**: Exact population-level identity: bias ∝ misclassification heterogeneity × CATE heterogeneity (Δπ×Δτ product form). NOT a bound — it is an exact identity under A1–A4; finite-sample R² measures MC noise, not bound looseness. Includes \"debiasing can hurt\" proposition with constructive example. Frame as \"bias decomposition\" or \"bias identity\" throughout — never \"bias bound.\" (Theory + Empirical)\n31\t2. **EC-HTE framework**: Hierarchical Bayesian subgroup-stratified misclassification correction + variance-adjusted honest causal forest. (Method)\n32\t3. **Diagnostic criterion**: Computable threshold from pilot data to decide whether stratified correction is needed. (Practical tool)\n33\t4. **EC-HTE + PPCI complementarity**: EC-HTE is a structured correction exploiting mixing matrix structure for covariate-side error; PPCI rectifier is a general-purpose residual correction that captures any systematic error (including outcome-side). They are complementary, not orthogonal — PPCI alone can partially correct covariate error but less efficiently than M⁻¹; in composition, EC-HTE handles the structured covariate error precisely, and the residual rectifier handles remaining outcome error. Composition achieves best-of-both when both error types are present. Elevate in abstract and contributions list. (Method + Empirical)\n34\t5. **Comprehensive validation**: Synthetic + semi-synthetic + real-data case study (pattern consistency, not ground-truth benchmark) with multiple LLMs. (Empirical)\n35\t\n36\t## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns\n37\t\n38\t1. **\"Why not just use expert labels directly for CATE?\"** — Defense: 2000 expert labels / k subgroups / honest forest halving → insufficient power per cell. EC-HTE uses all LLM data for low-variance CATE, expert labels only for calibration. Explicitly quantify in experiments.\n39\t\n40\t2. **\"How is this different from PPCI / DSL?\"** — PPCI rectifier is a general-purpose correction that captures any systematic error (covariate + outcome); EC-HTE is a structured correction exploiting mixing matrix structure specifically for covariate-side error. They are complementary: EC-HTE alone is more efficient for covariate error (uses M⁻¹ structure), PPCI alone can partially correct covariate error but with higher variance (limited by gold subset). In composition, EC-HTE handles structured covariate error, residual rectifier handles outcome error. DSL applied per-subgroup is a valid baseline but lacks cross-subgroup shrinkage, operates at estimand level rather than measurement model level, and has strictly higher variance under partial pooling. Do NOT say \"DSL cannot do subgroup correction\" — say it lacks the principled shrinkage mechanism.\n41\t\n42\t3. **\"Incremental over Mozer (2026)\"** — Mozer addresses ATE under differential prediction error. We address CATE under heterogeneous effect-modifier misclassification — a structurally different problem. The bias mechanism we identify (pooled correction hurting low-error subgroups) has no counterpart in Mozer's framework.\n43\t\n44\t4. **\"How sensitive to subgroup definition?\"** — Hierarchical Bayesian partial pooling makes the method robust: with well-defined subgroups, full stratification; with noisy subgroups, automatic shrinkage toward global. Include robustness analysis.\n45\t\n46\t5. **\"Real-world expert label cost\"** — exp-006 provides precise RMSE crossover points: K=2/extreme n_expert=250, K=4/extreme n=1000, K=4/moderate n=2000, K=2/moderate never crosses (global always better on RMSE). Paper needs \"Expert Budget Recommendation\" subsection with per-K guidelines. Key message: when diagnostic criterion flags high heterogeneity (extreme regime), EC-HTE is worth the investment at moderate budgets; when heterogeneity is low, global correction is the safer choice. This honest framing connects Claim 2 (EC-HTE method) to Claim 3 (diagnostic criterion) — the diagnostic decides the correction strategy.\n47\t\n48\t6. **\"How does this differ from Carroll et al. (2006) / classical measurement error?\"** — The qualitative insight that misspecified corrections can amplify MSE is classical (Fuller 1987, Sec. 2.3). Our specific contributions beyond this are: (a) the quantitative Δπ×Δτ product form for CATE estimation with effect-modifier misclassification, (b) demonstrating this failure mode occurs *by default* in standard LLM annotation workflows, and (c) providing a computable diagnostic and correction framework. Must explicitly cite and contextualize: Fuller (1987), Carroll et al. (2006 Ch. 4), Gustafson (2003), Hu & Schennach (2008), Imai & Yamamoto (2010). Also cite Bayesian annotation models: Dawid & Skene, Passonneau & Carpenter (2014), Hovy et al. (2013). **Bibliography target: 30-35 references** spanning measurement error, Bayesian estimation, causal inference, LLM-as-annotator (Tornberg 2024, Gilardi et al. 2023, Ziems et al. 2024), HTE methods (Wager & Athey 2018, Kennedy 2023, Chernozhukov et al. 2018).\n49\t\n50\t7. **\"Methodological novelty is modest\"** — Acknowledge explicitly. Position contribution as problem discovery + formal characterization + comprehensive validation, not as a new estimation technique. HB partial pooling provides meaningful RMSE reduction for moderate subgroups but is NOT \"never worse than global\" — it is a James-Stein shrinkage estimator with known boundary conditions. **Recommended condition**: n_expert per subgroup ≥ ~25 (e.g., n_expert ≥ 100 for K=4 with 12% minority). Below this, stratified MLE is preferred. This honest limitation strengthens credibility. State conditions explicitly using per-subgroup formulation, not just total n_expert.\n51\t\n52\t8. **\"Why no figures?\"** — Paper MUST include key figures: (1) schematic of bias mechanism (global over/under-correction), (2) bias amplification heatmap (Δπ vs Δτ), (3) diagnostic ROC curve, (4) budget crossover curves. Move P2 experiments (exp-008, exp-009) to appendix to free space.\n53\t\n54\t## Venue Fit\n55\t\n56\tEMNLP fit is strong: core NLP application (LLM annotation quality), computational social science use cases (sentiment analysis, hate speech detection, refugee interview coding), direct comparison with NeurIPS/EMNLP published methods (DSL, PPI, benchmarking). Methodology is causal inference but framed through NLP lens. Potential reviewer pool: CL+CSS track. **NOTE**: Framing broadened from \"political-science\" to \"CSS\" (D004b) — TweetEval stance tasks too small (N<1000), sentiment (N=45K) usable but not political science. CSS is more accurate and equally strong for EMNLP.\n57\t\n58\tRisk: pure statistics reviewers may want stronger theoretical contribution (convergence rates, minimax bounds). Mitigation: emphasize the practical diagnostic criterion and empirical validation breadth.\n59\t\n60\t## Writing Requirements (Dream Round 2 consensus)\n61\t\n62\t1. **\"Bias decomposition\" NOT \"bias bound\"** — Theorem 1 is an exact identity at population level. R² < 1.0 in verification reflects finite-sample MC noise, not bound looseness.\n63\t2. **Bibliography: 30-35 references minimum** — Must span 5 literatures: classical measurement error, Bayesian estimation, causal inference, LLM-as-annotator, CSS. Critical citations: Fuller 1987, Wager & Athey 2018, Imai & Yamamoto 2010.\n64\t3. **3+ figures in main text** — (a) bias mechanism schematic, (b) Δπ×Δτ amplification heatmap, (c) diagnostic ROC curve, (d) budget crossover curves. Move exp-008/exp-009 tables to appendix.\n65\t4. **Semi-synthetic DGP fully specified** — outcome generation, treatment assignment, CATE injection mechanism.\n66\t5. **Real-data framed as pattern consistency** — NOT ground-truth benchmark. No bias/RMSE against unknown true CATEs.\n67\t6. **EC-HTE advantage is conditional, not universal** — ec_hte dominates when misclassification heterogeneity is high (extreme regime) OR n_expert is large (≥500). In moderate regime with small n_expert (~100), global correction can outperform ec_hte because per-subgroup CM estimation noise exceeds the benefit of stratification. This connects to Claim 3: the diagnostic criterion (Δπ×Δτ) predicts when EC-HTE is needed. Unified condition: n_expert/K ≥ ~25-50 experts per subgroup.\n68\t11. **RMSE tradeoff must be disclosed** — ec_hte has higher RMSE than global/naive in K=4/n≤100 configs (up to 3.5x in extreme cases) due to noisy per-subgroup CM estimation. Bias is lower but variance is higher. Must report both bias AND RMSE; include bias-RMSE tradeoff analysis. At n_expert≥500, ec_hte RMSE becomes competitive.\n69\t12. **K-dependence of debiasing-hurts** — K=2 extreme magnification ~2.7x; K=4 extreme ~5.9x. More subgroups → larger π heterogeneity → more severe global correction damage. Report this scaling.\n70\t7. **Confusion matrix invertibility** — State det(Ĉ_k) ≠ 0 assumption explicitly.\n71\t8. **Diagnostic AUC cross-validated** — Leave-one-setting-out or held-out evaluation, not in-sample.\n72\t9. **Hyperprior values stated + sensitivity analysis** — Gamma(α, β) for Dirichlet concentration; report sensitivity to 2× changes.\n73\t10. **PPCI complementarity elevated** — Highlight in abstract and contributions. Frame as \"complementary\" not \"orthogonal\": EC-HTE is structured covariate-error correction; PPCI rectifier is general residual correction. Composition = EC-HTE handles structured part + rectifier handles remainder (primarily outcome error).\n74\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"161acbfe-115e-4501-915e-b96cb773dc21","uuid":"67a669fc-d96f-4c03-913d-b18089f79a13","timestamp":"2026-05-21T04:03:25.324Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520/projects/ec_hte_llm_annotation/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T032758/worst/paper_framing.md","content":"# Paper Framing — ec_hte_llm_annotation\n\n## Target Venue\n- Primary: EMNLP 2026 Main (via ARR)\n- Deadline: ARR submission May 25, 2026; commitment deadline August 2, 2026\n- Format: Long paper, 8 pages + unlimited references\n\n## Core Claims (3 条，可证伪)\n\n1. **Bias Mechanism Discovery**: When LLM misclassification rates for an effect modifier vary across subgroups, applying a global (pooled) correction to subgroup-level CATE estimates introduces systematic bias — over-correcting low-error subgroups and under-correcting high-error ones. In realistic settings, this \"debiasing\" can worsen CATE bias by 2-3x (median across configurations), with extreme cases exceeding 20x. Global correction increases subgroup-level bias in ~50% of the parameter space.\n\n2. **EC-HTE Solution**: Hierarchical Bayesian subgroup-stratified correction (EC-HTE) substantially reduces subgroup-level bias while controlling estimation variance through partial pooling. HB provides meaningful RMSE reduction in moderate-sized subgroups by borrowing strength across groups (classic James-Stein shrinkage). **Boundary condition**: when expert labels per subgroup < ~25 (e.g., n_expert=50 with 12% minority subgroup → ~6 labels) AND subgroup parameter is far from group mean, shrinkage can be harmful — stratified MLE is preferred. Recommended: n_expert such that smallest subgroup contains ≥25 expert labels. **NOTE**: exact improvement magnitudes pending full 100 MC confirmation (current estimates from n_mc=2 dry-run). Demonstrated with both difference-in-means and CausalForestDML (exp-011: consistent bias reduction pattern, ec_hte_forest |bias|=0.06 vs naive 0.16). CausalForest amplifies the debiasing-hurts effect (up to 5.9x in K=4/extreme) due to nonlinear CATE estimation.\n\n3. **Diagnostic Criterion**: The product of misclassification heterogeneity × CATE heterogeneity across subgroups provides a computable diagnostic: when this product exceeds a threshold, global correction is harmful and stratified correction is needed. This criterion has standalone value independent of EC-HTE.\n\n## Story Spine\n\n- **Problem**: NLP researchers increasingly use LLM annotations as effect modifiers in heterogeneous treatment effect analysis across computational social science (CSS) — e.g., sentiment framing moderates message effectiveness, hate speech type moderates intervention impact. LLM misclassification is non-random — error rates vary by subgroup (Ashwin et al., 2025: error varies by refugee status, gender, education; documented across CSS tasks including sentiment, stance, toxicity). Current PPI/DSL corrections apply global rectifiers assuming homogeneous error.\n\n- **Existing approaches & gaps**: Mozer (2026) proves arm-specific rectifiers fix ATE bias under differential prediction error, but explicitly limits analysis to average treatment effects. PPI (Angelopoulos et al., 2023) and DSL (Egami et al., 2024) correct population-level estimates. PPCI (Sui et al., 2026) extends PPI to conditional functionals but corrects outcome-side measurement, not covariate-side. ForestIV (Bu et al., 2023) handles ML-covariate measurement error in regression, not CATE. **No work addresses correction when the mismeasured variable defines the heterogeneity structure.**\n\n- **Our framing**: We identify and formalize a previously unrecognized bias mechanism — measurement-error × causal-heterogeneity confounding. When a pooled confusion matrix is applied to correct subgroup-level CATEs, it misattributes prediction noise across subgroups with different error rates, producing bias proportional to the product of misclassification heterogeneity and CATE heterogeneity. This is a problem-level discovery, not a technique-level extension. EC-HTE solves it via hierarchical Bayesian stratified correction with honest causal forest integration. **Novelty positioning**: The individual components (HB Dirichlet estimation, confusion matrix correction, causal forests) are established; the contribution is (a) identifying and formalizing the bias mechanism specific to effect-modifier misclassification, (b) the Δπ×Δτ product characterization that predicts when correction backfires, and (c) bridging the measurement error and HTE literatures in a practically consequential way. Frame as \"problem identification + principled solution\" not \"new estimation technique.\"\n\n- **Headline result template**: \"On semi-synthetic CSS benchmarks (sentiment analysis, hate speech detection), global correction worsens subgroup CATE bias by 2-3x (median), with up to 20x in extreme cases; EC-HTE reduces bias to near-zero while maintaining 92-97% coverage, with as few as 250 expert labels.\"\n\n- **Oracle framing**: When reporting DGP C results where ec_hte_ppci outperforms oracle, always use \"covariate-oracle\" (true Z + observed Y), never unqualified \"oracle\". Frame as \"composite correction is necessary and sufficient when both error types coexist\" — NOT \"beats oracle\". The result is by construction (ec_hte_ppci corrects both error types; covariate-oracle only corrects one), not surprising.\n\n## Key Contributions (ordered for paper)\n\n1. **Bias decomposition identity**: Exact population-level identity: bias ∝ misclassification heterogeneity × CATE heterogeneity (Δπ×Δτ product form). NOT a bound — it is an exact identity under A1–A4; finite-sample R² measures MC noise, not bound looseness. Includes \"debiasing can hurt\" proposition with constructive example. Frame as \"bias decomposition\" or \"bias identity\" throughout — never \"bias bound.\" (Theory + Empirical)\n2. **EC-HTE framework**: Hierarchical Bayesian subgroup-stratified misclassification correction + variance-adjusted honest causal forest. (Method)\n3. **Diagnostic criterion**: Computable threshold from pilot data to decide whether stratified correction is needed. (Practical tool)\n4. **EC-HTE + PPCI complementarity**: EC-HTE is a structured correction exploiting mixing matrix structure for covariate-side error; PPCI rectifier is a general-purpose residual correction that captures any systematic error (including outcome-side). They are complementary, not orthogonal — PPCI alone can partially correct covariate error but less efficiently than M⁻¹; in composition, EC-HTE handles the structured covariate error precisely, and the residual rectifier handles remaining outcome error. Composition achieves best-of-both when both error types are present. Elevate in abstract and contributions list. (Method + Empirical)\n5. **Comprehensive validation**: Synthetic + semi-synthetic + real-data case study (pattern consistency, not ground-truth benchmark) with multiple LLMs. (Empirical)\n\n## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns\n\n1. **\"Why not just use expert labels directly for CATE?\"** — Defense: 2000 expert labels / k subgroups / honest forest halving → insufficient power per cell. EC-HTE uses all LLM data for low-variance CATE, expert labels only for calibration. Explicitly quantify in experiments.\n\n2. **\"How is this different from PPCI / DSL?\"** — PPCI rectifier is a general-purpose correction that captures any systematic error (covariate + outcome); EC-HTE is a structured correction exploiting mixing matrix structure specifically for covariate-side error. They are complementary: EC-HTE alone is more efficient for covariate error (uses M⁻¹ structure), PPCI alone can partially correct covariate error but with higher variance (limited by gold subset). In composition, EC-HTE handles structured covariate error, residual rectifier handles outcome error. DSL applied per-subgroup is a valid baseline but lacks cross-subgroup shrinkage, operates at estimand level rather than measurement model level, and has strictly higher variance under partial pooling. Do NOT say \"DSL cannot do subgroup correction\" — say it lacks the principled shrinkage mechanism.\n\n3. **\"Incremental over Mozer (2026)\"** — Mozer addresses ATE under differential prediction error. We address CATE under heterogeneous effect-modifier misclassification — a structurally different problem. The bias mechanism we identify (pooled correction hurting low-error subgroups) has no counterpart in Mozer's framework.\n\n4. **\"How sensitive to subgroup definition?\"** — Hierarchical Bayesian partial pooling makes the method robust: with well-defined subgroups, full stratification; with noisy subgroups, automatic shrinkage toward global. Include robustness analysis.\n\n5. **\"Real-world expert label cost\"** — exp-006 provides precise RMSE crossover points: K=2/extreme n_expert=250, K=4/extreme n=1000, K=4/moderate n=2000, K=2/moderate never crosses (global always better on RMSE). Paper needs \"Expert Budget Recommendation\" subsection with per-K guidelines. Key message: when diagnostic criterion flags high heterogeneity (extreme regime), EC-HTE is worth the investment at moderate budgets; when heterogeneity is low, global correction is the safer choice. This honest framing connects Claim 2 (EC-HTE method) to Claim 3 (diagnostic criterion) — the diagnostic decides the correction strategy.\n\n6. **\"How does this differ from Carroll et al. (2006) / classical measurement error?\"** — The qualitative insight that misspecified corrections can amplify MSE is classical (Fuller 1987, Sec. 2.3). Our specific contributions beyond this are: (a) the quantitative Δπ×Δτ product form for CATE estimation with effect-modifier misclassification, (b) demonstrating this failure mode occurs *by default* in standard LLM annotation workflows, and (c) providing a computable diagnostic and correction framework. Must explicitly cite and contextualize: Fuller (1987), Carroll et al. (2006 Ch. 4), Gustafson (2003), Hu & Schennach (2008), Imai & Yamamoto (2010). Also cite Bayesian annotation models: Dawid & Skene, Passonneau & Carpenter (2014), Hovy et al. (2013). **Bibliography target: 30-35 references** spanning measurement error, Bayesian estimation, causal inference, LLM-as-annotator (Tornberg 2024, Gilardi et al. 2023, Ziems et al. 2024), HTE methods (Wager & Athey 2018, Kennedy 2023, Chernozhukov et al. 2018).\n\n7. **\"Methodological novelty is modest\"** — Acknowledge explicitly. Position contribution as problem discovery + formal characterization + comprehensive validation, not as a new estimation technique. HB partial pooling provides meaningful RMSE reduction for moderate subgroups but is NOT \"never worse than global\" — it is a James-Stein shrinkage estimator with known boundary conditions. **Recommended condition**: n_expert per subgroup ≥ ~25 (e.g., n_expert ≥ 100 for K=4 with 12% minority). Below this, stratified MLE is preferred. This honest limitation strengthens credibility. State conditions explicitly using per-subgroup formulation, not just total n_expert.\n\n8. **\"Why no figures?\"** — Paper MUST include key figures: (1) schematic of bias mechanism (global over/under-correction), (2) bias amplification heatmap (Δπ vs Δτ), (3) diagnostic ROC curve, (4) budget crossover curves. Move P2 experiments (exp-008, exp-009) to appendix to free space.\n\n## Venue Fit\n\nEMNLP fit is strong: core NLP application (LLM annotation quality), computational social science use cases (sentiment analysis, hate speech detection, refugee interview coding), direct comparison with NeurIPS/EMNLP published methods (DSL, PPI, benchmarking). Methodology is causal inference but framed through NLP lens. Potential reviewer pool: CL+CSS track. **NOTE**: Framing broadened from \"political-science\" to \"CSS\" (D004b) — TweetEval stance tasks too small (N<1000), sentiment (N=45K) usable but not political science. CSS is more accurate and equally strong for EMNLP.\n\nRisk: pure statistics reviewers may want stronger theoretical contribution (convergence rates, minimax bounds). Mitigation: emphasize the practical diagnostic criterion and empirical validation breadth.\n\n## Writing Requirements (Dream Round 2 consensus)\n\n1. **\"Bias decomposition\" NOT \"bias bound\"** — Theorem 1 is an exact identity at population level. R² < 1.0 in verification reflects finite-sample MC noise, not bound looseness.\n2. **Bibliography: 30-35 references minimum** — Must span 5 literatures: classical measurement error, Bayesian estimation, causal inference, LLM-as-annotator, CSS. Critical citations: Fuller 1987, Wager & Athey 2018, Imai & Yamamoto 2010.\n3. **3+ figures in main text** — (a) bias mechanism schematic, (b) Δπ×Δτ amplification heatmap, (c) diagnostic ROC curve, (d) budget crossover curves. Move exp-008/exp-009 tables to appendix.\n4. **Semi-synthetic DGP fully specified** — outcome generation, treatment assignment, CATE injection mechanism.\n5. **Real-data framed as pattern consistency** — NOT ground-truth benchmark. No bias/RMSE against unknown true CATEs.\n6. **EC-HTE advantage is conditional, not universal** — ec_hte dominates when misclassification heterogeneity is high (extreme regime) OR n_expert is large (≥500). In moderate regime with small n_expert (~100), global correction can outperform ec_hte because per-subgroup CM estimation noise exceeds the benefit of stratification. This connects to Claim 3: the diagnostic criterion (Δπ×Δτ) predicts when EC-HTE is needed. Unified condition: n_expert/K ≥ ~25-50 experts per subgroup.\n11. **RMSE tradeoff must be disclosed** — ec_hte has higher RMSE than global/naive in K=4/n≤100 configs (up to 3.5x in extreme cases) due to noisy per-subgroup CM estimation. Bias is lower but variance is higher. Must report both bias AND RMSE; include bias-RMSE tradeoff analysis. At n_expert≥500, ec_hte RMSE becomes competitive.\n12. **K-dependence of debiasing-hurts** — K=2 extreme magnification ~2.7x; K=4 extreme ~5.9x. More subgroups → larger π heterogeneity → more severe global correction damage. Report this scaling.\n7. **Confusion matrix invertibility** — State det(Ĉ_k) ≠ 0 assumption explicitly.\n8. **Diagnostic AUC cross-validated** — Leave-one-setting-out or held-out evaluation, not in-sample.\n9. **Hyperprior values stated + sensitivity analysis** — Gamma(α, β) for Dirichlet concentration; report sensitivity to 2× changes.\n10. **PPCI complementarity elevated** — Highlight in abstract and contributions. Frame as \"complementary\" not \"orthogonal\": EC-HTE is structured covariate-error correction; PPCI rectifier is general residual correction. Composition = EC-HTE handles structured part + rectifier handles remainder (primarily outcome error).\n","numLines":74,"startLine":1,"totalLines":74}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011vACRdX9xUceeTbx76E1PX","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t[\n2\t  {\n3\t    \"name\": \"Real-data case study — UC Berkeley Measuring Hate Speech\",\n4\t    \"description\": \"Apply EC-HTE to UC Berkeley hate speech corpus with demographic subgroups (race, gender, age, education). Annotate with GPT-4.1 via API. Compare global-corrected vs EC-HTE CATE estimates. Frame as 'Audit of Current Practice' — show that when LLM errors are homogeneous, global and EC-HTE converge, confirming theory predicts when correction is (un)necessary.\\n\",\n5\t    \"results\": {\n6\t      \"llm_overall_accuracy\": 0.82,\n7\t      \"llm_delta_pi_max_across_demographic_subgroups\": 0.03,\n8\t      \"llm_delta_pi_race\": 0.02,\n9\t      \"llm_delta_pi_gender\": 0.01,\n10\t      \"llm_delta_pi_age\": 0.04,\n11\t      \"llm_delta_pi_education\": 0.03,\n12\t      \"global_vs_ec_hte_max_cate_shift_abs\": 0.018,\n13\t      \"global_vs_ec_hte_max_cate_shift_se\": 0.35,\n14\t      \"global_vs_ec_hte_mean_cate_shift_se\": 0.18,\n15\t      \"num_subgroups_shift_exceeds_1se\": 0,\n16\t      \"num_subgroups_shift_exceeds_0_5se\": 1,\n17\t      \"ec_hte_vs_global_qualitative_agreement\": true,\n18\t      \"bootstrap_ci_overlap_pct\": 100,\n19\t      \"domain_knowledge_consistency\": \"both methods agree on direction of demographic effects\",\n20\t      \"expert_labels_used\": 500,\n21\t      \"n_total_llm_annotated\": 39565\n22\t    },\n23\t    \"tables\": [\n24\t      {\n25\t        \"name\": \"Table: Real-Data CATE Estimates by Demographic Subgroup\",\n26\t        \"columns\": [\n27\t          \"subgroup\",\n28\t          \"n\",\n29\t          \"pi_hat_global\",\n30\t          \"pi_hat_subgroup\",\n31\t          \"cate_naive\",\n32\t          \"cate_global\",\n33\t          \"cate_ec_hte\",\n34\t          \"shift_se\",\n35\t          \"bootstrap_95ci_overlap\"\n36\t        ],\n37\t        \"rows\": [\n38\t          [\n39\t            \"race_white\",\n40\t            18200,\n41\t            0.17,\n42\t            0.16,\n43\t            0.031,\n44\t            0.028,\n45\t            0.027,\n46\t            0.15,\n47\t            true\n48\t          ],\n49\t          [\n50\t            \"race_nonwhite\",\n51\t            21365,\n52\t            0.17,\n53\t            0.18,\n54\t            0.044,\n55\t            0.04,\n56\t            0.041,\n57\t            0.22,\n58\t            true\n59\t          ],\n60\t          [\n61\t            \"gender_male\",\n62\t            20100,\n63\t            0.17,\n64\t            0.17,\n65\t            0.039,\n66\t            0.035,\n67\t            0.035,\n68\t            0.05,\n69\t            true\n70\t          ],\n71\t          [\n72\t            \"gender_female\",\n73\t            19465,\n74\t            0.17,\n75\t            0.18,\n76\t            0.036,\n77\t            0.033,\n78\t            0.034,\n79\t            0.18,\n80\t            true\n81\t          ],\n82\t          [\n83\t            \"age_under30\",\n84\t            15800,\n85\t            0.17,\n86\t            0.16,\n87\t            0.042,\n88\t            0.038,\n89\t            0.037,\n90\t            0.2,\n91\t            true\n92\t          ],\n93\t          [\n94\t            \"age_30plus\",\n95\t            23765,\n96\t            0.17,\n97\t            0.19,\n98\t            0.033,\n99\t            0.03,\n100\t            0.031,\n101\t            0.28,\n102\t            true\n103\t          ],\n104\t          [\n105\t            \"edu_college\",\n106\t            22100,\n107\t            0.17,\n108\t            0.17,\n109\t            0.037,\n110\t            0.034,\n111\t            0.034,\n112\t            0.08,\n113\t            true\n114\t          ],\n115\t          [\n116\t            \"edu_no_college\",\n117\t            17465,\n118\t            0.17,\n119\t            0.2,\n120\t            0.038,\n121\t            0.034,\n122\t            0.036,\n123\t            0.35,\n124\t            true\n125\t          ]\n126\t        ]\n127\t      },\n128\t      {\n129\t        \"name\": \"Table: Misclassification Heterogeneity Summary\",\n130\t        \"columns\": [\n131\t          \"demographic_dimension\",\n132\t          \"n_subgroups\",\n133\t          \"max_delta_pi\",\n134\t          \"min_pi\",\n135\t          \"max_pi\",\n136\t          \"heterogeneity_regime\"\n137\t        ],\n138\t        \"rows\": [\n139\t          [\n140\t            \"race\",\n141\t            2,\n142\t            0.02,\n143\t            0.16,\n144\t            0.18,\n145\t            \"homogeneous\"\n146\t          ],\n147\t          [\n148\t            \"gender\",\n149\t            2,\n150\t            0.01,\n151\t            0.17,\n152\t            0.18,\n153\t            \"homogeneous\"\n154\t          ],\n155\t          [\n156\t            \"age\",\n157\t            2,\n158\t            0.04,\n159\t            0.16,\n160\t            0.19,\n161\t            \"homogeneous\"\n162\t          ],\n163\t          [\n164\t            \"education\",\n165\t            2,\n166\t            0.03,\n167\t            0.17,\n168\t            0.2,\n169\t            \"homogeneous\"\n170\t          ]\n171\t        ]\n172\t      }\n173\t    ],\n174\t    \"figures\": [],\n175\t    \"analysis\": \"The real-data audit confirms the worst-tier prediction: on a well-studied English CSS task, SOTA LLMs produce near-homogeneous errors across demographic subgroups (max Δπ = 0.04 for age). Consequently, global and EC-HTE CATE estimates are statistically indistinguishable — the largest shift is 0.35 SE (education dimension), with 100% bootstrap CI overlap across all subgroups. This result has TWO interpretations for the paper: (1) Negative: EC-HTE provides no practical benefit on this dataset, undermining claims of immediate applicability. (2) Positive (reframed): The theory correctly predicts that correction is unnecessary when Δπ ≈ 0, validating the diagnostic framework. Frame as 'the bias identity correctly identifies the homogeneous regime.' Causal framework mapping: T = exposure to hate speech content, Y = perceived severity rating, Z = hate speech type (LLM-classified), S = demographic subgroup. Note limitation: causal interpretation is illustrative, not designed for rigorous causal inference on this observational corpus. CRITICAL PIVOT: use this result to motivate the 'Characterizing the Homogeneity Boundary' analysis — at what Δπ does the mechanism activate? Back-calculate from the bias identity: for bias > 0.05 (practical threshold), need Δπ > ~0.12 given observed Δτ. This characterization is itself a contribution — it tells practitioners when to worry.\\n\\n\\nGPT-4.1 achieves uniformly high accuracy on English hate speech classification — a well-studied CSS task with abundant training data. Demographic subgroups (race, gender, age, education of annotator or target) produce Δπ ≤ 0.04, well below the threshold where the bias mechanism becomes practically relevant. With near-homogeneous confusion matrices across subgroups, the global pooled CM is an adequate approximation of each subgroup CM, so global correction and EC-HTE produce statistically indistinguishable CATE estimates. All point estimate shifts fall within 0.35 SE — not actionable. This is exactly the prediction of the bias identity: when Δπ ≈ 0, bias ∝ Δπ×Δτ ≈ 0 regardless of CATE heterogeneity. The result is a negative finding for practical relevance but a positive finding for theory validation — the framework correctly identifies the homogeneous regime as safe for global correction.\\n\"\n176\t  },\n177\t  {\n178\t    \"name\": \"Multiple LLM annotator comparison\",\n179\t    \"description\": \"Annotate TweetEval sentiment with GPT-4.1 (API), Qwen3-235B-A22B (GPU), and Claude Sonnet 4.6 (API). Measure per-subgroup misclassification rates for each LLM. Compare EC-HTE benefit across LLMs of different quality tiers.\\n\",\n180\t    \"results\": {\n181\t      \"gpt4_1_overall_accuracy\": 0.88,\n182\t      \"gpt4_1_delta_pi_max\": 0.03,\n183\t      \"gpt4_1_bias_amplification_median\": 1.08,\n184\t      \"qwen3_overall_accuracy\": 0.81,\n185\t      \"qwen3_delta_pi_max\": 0.05,\n186\t      \"qwen3_bias_amplification_median\": 1.22,\n187\t      \"claude_sonnet_overall_accuracy\": 0.86,\n188\t      \"claude_sonnet_delta_pi_max\": 0.04,\n189\t      \"claude_sonnet_bias_amplification_median\": 1.14,\n190\t      \"ec_hte_rmse_ratio_vs_global_gpt4_1_n500_k4\": 2.1,\n191\t      \"ec_hte_rmse_ratio_vs_global_qwen3_n500_k4\": 1.7,\n192\t      \"ec_hte_rmse_ratio_vs_global_claude_n500_k4\": 1.9,\n193\t      \"ec_hte_bias_reduction_pct_gpt4_1\": 8,\n194\t      \"ec_hte_bias_reduction_pct_qwen3\": 22,\n195\t      \"ec_hte_bias_reduction_pct_claude\": 14,\n196\t      \"all_llms_homogeneous_regime\": true,\n197\t      \"n_samples_annotated_per_llm\": 5000,\n198\t      \"annotation_cost_gpt4_1_usd\": 12,\n199\t      \"annotation_cost_claude_usd\": 18,\n200\t      \"gpu_hours_qwen3\": 8\n201\t    },\n202\t    \"tables\": [\n203\t      {\n204\t        \"name\": \"Table: LLM Comparison — Misclassification Heterogeneity\",\n205\t        \"columns\": [\n206\t          \"llm\",\n207\t          \"accuracy\",\n208\t          \"delta_pi_max\",\n209\t          \"subgroup_with_max_delta\",\n210\t          \"bias_amp_median\",\n211\t          \"bias_amp_max\",\n212\t          \"regime\"\n213\t        ],\n214\t        \"rows\": [\n215\t          [\n216\t            \"GPT-4.1\",\n217\t            0.88,\n218\t            0.03,\n219\t            \"has_hashtag\",\n220\t            1.08,\n221\t            1.15,\n222\t            \"homogeneous\"\n223\t          ],\n224\t          [\n225\t            \"Qwen3-235B\",\n226\t            0.81,\n227\t            0.05,\n228\t            \"short_text\",\n229\t            1.22,\n230\t            1.29,\n231\t            \"homogeneous\"\n232\t          ],\n233\t          [\n234\t            \"Claude-Sonnet-4.6\",\n235\t            0.86,\n236\t            0.04,\n237\t            \"has_hashtag\",\n238\t            1.14,\n239\t            1.21,\n240\t            \"homogeneous\"\n241\t          ]\n242\t        ]\n243\t      },\n244\t      {\n245\t        \"name\": \"Table: EC-HTE vs Global by LLM (n_expert=500, K=4)\",\n246\t        \"columns\": [\n247\t          \"llm\",\n248\t          \"method\",\n249\t          \"mean_abs_bias\",\n250\t          \"rmse\",\n251\t          \"coverage_95\",\n252\t          \"bias_reduction_pct\",\n253\t          \"rmse_ratio_vs_global\"\n254\t        ],\n255\t        \"rows\": [\n256\t          [\n257\t            \"GPT-4.1\",\n258\t            \"global\",\n259\t            0.012,\n260\t            0.089,\n261\t            0.94,\n262\t            \"-\",\n263\t            1.0\n264\t          ],\n265\t          [\n266\t            \"GPT-4.1\",\n267\t            \"ec_hte\",\n268\t            0.011,\n269\t            0.187,\n270\t            0.86,\n271\t            8,\n272\t            2.1\n273\t          ],\n274\t          [\n275\t            \"Qwen3-235B\",\n276\t            \"global\",\n277\t            0.028,\n278\t            0.095,\n279\t            0.92,\n280\t            \"-\",\n281\t            1.0\n282\t          ],\n283\t          [\n284\t            \"Qwen3-235B\",\n285\t            \"ec_hte\",\n286\t            0.022,\n287\t            0.162,\n288\t            0.84,\n289\t            22,\n290\t            1.7\n291\t          ],\n292\t          [\n293\t            \"Claude-Sonnet-4.6\",\n294\t            \"global\",\n295\t            0.019,\n296\t            0.091,\n297\t            0.93,\n298\t            \"-\",\n299\t            1.0\n300\t          ],\n301\t          [\n302\t            \"Claude-Sonnet-4.6\",\n303\t            \"ec_hte\",\n304\t            0.016,\n305\t            0.173,\n306\t            0.85,\n307\t            14,\n308\t            1.9\n309\t          ]\n310\t        ]\n311\t      }\n312\t    ],\n313\t    \"figures\": [],\n314\t    \"analysis\": \"The multi-LLM comparison reveals a compressed quality spectrum on TweetEval sentiment: even the weakest model (Qwen3, 0.81 accuracy) achieves Δπ_max = 0.05, barely above the homogeneity floor. This confirms the worst-tier hypothesis that standard English CSS tasks are 'solved enough' that misclassification heterogeneity is negligible. EC-HTE never dominates global on RMSE for any LLM. The reframing implication: move this table to appendix, use it to support the 'current SOTA is in the homogeneous regime' argument. The key forward-looking claim becomes: as researchers apply LLMs to harder tasks (cross-lingual, low-resource, domain-specialized coding — per Ashwin et al. 2025), Δπ will increase and the mechanism will activate. The multi-LLM comparison shows the mechanism is 'dormant' on easy tasks, not 'absent' — Qwen3's slightly higher Δπ hints at the direction of the gradient.\\n\\n\\nAll three SOTA LLMs achieve high accuracy on TweetEval sentiment (a 3-class task with well-defined categories: positive/negative/neutral). English sentiment is among the easiest CSS tasks — abundant training data, clear label semantics, minimal cultural ambiguity. GPT-4.1 leads (0.88 accuracy) with the smallest Δπ (0.03); Qwen3 is weakest (0.81) with slightly larger Δπ (0.05) because its English performance lags frontier models, but 0.05 is still in the homogeneous regime per the bias identity. Claude Sonnet falls between. All three LLMs produce Δπ < 0.05, confirming the worst-tier assumption that SOTA LLMs on standard English CSS tasks are 'too accurate and too uniform for heterogeneity to matter.' Bias amplification ranges 1.08–1.22x — within MC noise, not practically meaningful. EC-HTE has strictly worse RMSE than global for all three LLMs at n_expert ≤ 2000 because the per-subgroup CM estimation variance exceeds the (tiny) bias reduction. Even the weakest LLM (Qwen3) only shows 22% bias reduction with 1.7x RMSE penalty — a bad tradeoff. This result means the 'LLM quality spectrum' story partially collapses: the spectrum exists but is compressed into a narrow band where EC-HTE is never the right choice on this task.\\n\"\n315\t  },\n316\t  {\n317\t    \"name\": \"Adaptive expert annotation allocation\",\n318\t    \"description\": \"Implement CATE-weighted allocation targeting high-CATE × high-error subgroups. Compare against uniform and uncertainty-only allocation on synthetic data (where mechanism is active) and semi-synthetic with real LLM annotations (where mechanism is dormant).\\n\",\n319\t    \"results\": {\n320\t      \"synth_extreme_adaptive_vs_uniform_rmse_reduction_k2\": 0.18,\n321\t      \"synth_extreme_adaptive_vs_uniform_rmse_reduction_k4\": 0.24,\n322\t      \"synth_extreme_adaptive_vs_uncertainty_rmse_reduction_k4\": 0.09,\n323\t      \"synth_extreme_optimal_allocation_ratio_high_error_sg\": 2.8,\n324\t      \"semi_synth_llm_adaptive_vs_uniform_rmse_reduction_k4\": 0.02,\n325\t      \"semi_synth_llm_adaptive_vs_uniform_bias_reduction\": 0.003,\n326\t      \"semi_synth_llm_allocation_entropy\": 0.97,\n327\t      \"convergence_iterations\": 3,\n328\t      \"allocation_stability_across_seeds\": 0.91,\n329\t      \"n_expert_total\": 500\n330\t    },\n331\t    \"tables\": [\n332\t      {\n333\t        \"name\": \"Table: Allocation Strategy Comparison (K=4, n_expert=500)\",\n334\t        \"columns\": [\n335\t          \"data_source\",\n336\t          \"allocation\",\n337\t          \"mean_abs_bias\",\n338\t          \"rmse\",\n339\t          \"bias_vs_uniform_pct\",\n340\t          \"rmse_vs_uniform_pct\"\n341\t        ],\n342\t        \"rows\": [\n343\t          [\n344\t            \"synthetic_extreme\",\n345\t            \"uniform\",\n346\t            0.062,\n347\t            0.198,\n348\t            0,\n349\t            0\n350\t          ],\n351\t          [\n352\t            \"synthetic_extreme\",\n353\t            \"uncertainty_only\",\n354\t            0.055,\n355\t            0.178,\n356\t            -11,\n357\t            -10\n358\t          ],\n359\t          [\n360\t            \"synthetic_extreme\",\n361\t            \"cate_weighted\",\n362\t            0.048,\n363\t            0.151,\n364\t            -23,\n365\t            -24\n366\t          ],\n367\t          [\n368\t            \"synthetic_moderate\",\n369\t            \"uniform\",\n370\t            0.031,\n371\t            0.145,\n372\t            0,\n373\t            0\n374\t          ],\n375\t          [\n376\t            \"synthetic_moderate\",\n377\t            \"cate_weighted\",\n378\t            0.027,\n379\t            0.131,\n380\t            -13,\n381\t            -10\n382\t          ],\n383\t          [\n384\t            \"semi_synth_llm\",\n385\t            \"uniform\",\n386\t            0.018,\n387\t            0.142,\n388\t            0,\n389\t            0\n390\t          ],\n391\t          [\n392\t            \"semi_synth_llm\",\n393\t            \"cate_weighted\",\n394\t            0.017,\n395\t            0.139,\n396\t            -6,\n397\t            -2\n398\t          ]\n399\t        ]\n400\t      },\n401\t      {\n402\t        \"name\": \"Table: Allocation Weights by Subgroup (K=4, n_expert=500)\",\n403\t        \"columns\": [\n404\t          \"data_source\",\n405\t          \"subgroup\",\n406\t          \"true_pi\",\n407\t          \"true_cate\",\n408\t          \"uniform_weight\",\n409\t          \"adaptive_weight\"\n410\t        ],\n411\t        \"rows\": [\n412\t          [\n413\t            \"synthetic_extreme\",\n414\t            \"S0\",\n415\t            0.05,\n416\t            0.1,\n417\t            0.25,\n418\t            0.12\n419\t          ],\n420\t          [\n421\t            \"synthetic_extreme\",\n422\t            \"S1\",\n423\t            0.1,\n424\t            0.1,\n425\t            0.25,\n426\t            0.18\n427\t          ],\n428\t          [\n429\t            \"synthetic_extreme\",\n430\t            \"S2\",\n431\t            0.2,\n432\t            0.3,\n433\t            0.25,\n434\t            0.28\n435\t          ],\n436\t          [\n437\t            \"synthetic_extreme\",\n438\t            \"S3\",\n439\t            0.3,\n440\t            0.3,\n441\t            0.25,\n442\t            0.42\n443\t          ],\n444\t          [\n445\t            \"semi_synth_llm\",\n446\t            \"S0\",\n447\t            0.12,\n448\t            0.1,\n449\t            0.25,\n450\t            0.24\n451\t          ],\n452\t          [\n453\t            \"semi_synth_llm\",\n454\t            \"S1\",\n455\t            0.13,\n456\t            0.1,\n457\t            0.25,\n458\t            0.25\n459\t          ],\n460\t          [\n461\t            \"semi_synth_llm\",\n462\t            \"S2\",\n463\t            0.14,\n464\t            0.3,\n465\t            0.25,\n466\t            0.26\n467\t          ],\n468\t          [\n469\t            \"semi_synth_llm\",\n470\t            \"S3\",\n471\t            0.15,\n472\t            0.3,\n473\t            0.25,\n474\t            0.25\n475\t          ]\n476\t        ]\n477\t      }\n478\t    ],\n479\t    \"figures\": [],\n480\t    \"analysis\": \"Adaptive allocation is a Stage 3 / P2 experiment that, under worst-tier conditions, provides negligible empirical value with real LLM annotations. The fundamental issue: when Δπ is small, the signal that should guide allocation (error rate × CATE magnitude) is buried in estimation noise. The algorithm converges in 3 iterations but converges to near-uniform because it correctly identifies that no subgroup has substantially more to gain. For the paper reframe: mention adaptive allocation as a theoretical extension in the Discussion section ('when the mechanism activates, practitioners can further optimize expert budget allocation'). Do not include in main experimental results. Synthetic proof-of-concept in appendix is sufficient.\\n\\n\\nOn synthetic data where Δπ is large (extreme regime), adaptive allocation works as designed: directing more expert labels to high-error × high-CATE subgroups reduces RMSE by 18-24% over uniform allocation because those subgroups have the most to gain from better CM estimation. However, on semi-synthetic with real LLM annotations (worst-tier: Δπ < 0.05), adaptive allocation degenerates to near-uniform (allocation entropy 0.97 vs 1.0 for perfect uniform) because all subgroups have similarly low error rates and similarly uncertain CATEs. The CATE-weighting signal is dominated by estimation noise when true Δπ is small, so the algorithm cannot reliably identify which subgroups benefit most. RMSE improvement is 2% — within MC noise. This makes adaptive allocation a theoretically valid but practically irrelevant contribution under worst-tier conditions. Recommendation: present synthetic results in appendix as proof-of-concept; do not feature in main text.\\n\"\n481\t  },\n482\t  {\n483\t    \"name\": \"Robustness to misspecified subgroup structure\",\n484\t    \"description\": \"Test EC-HTE when subgroup definitions are noisy (random 10-30% mislabeling of subgroup membership), overlapping (soft subgroup assignment), or when the true DGP has a different subgroup structure than the conditioning variable used by EC-HTE.\\n\",\n485\t    \"results\": {\n486\t      \"noise_10pct_ec_hte_rmse_ratio_vs_clean\": 1.12,\n487\t      \"noise_20pct_ec_hte_rmse_ratio_vs_clean\": 1.31,\n488\t      \"noise_30pct_ec_hte_rmse_ratio_vs_clean\": 1.58,\n489\t      \"noise_10pct_ec_hte_still_beats_global_bias\": true,\n490\t      \"noise_20pct_ec_hte_still_beats_global_bias\": true,\n491\t      \"noise_30pct_ec_hte_worse_than_global_rmse\": true,\n492\t      \"misspec_k2_for_k4_ec_hte_bias_reduction_vs_naive\": 0.35,\n493\t      \"misspec_k2_for_k4_ec_hte_bias_reduction_vs_global\": 0.1,\n494\t      \"misspec_k2_for_k4_rmse_penalty\": 1.4,\n495\t      \"misspec_discrete_for_continuous_ec_hte_bias_reduction\": 0.45,\n496\t      \"misspec_discrete_for_continuous_rmse_penalty\": 1.2,\n497\t      \"hb_vs_mle_robustness_advantage_noise_20pct\": 0.08,\n498\t      \"hb_shrinkage_mitigates_noise\": true,\n499\t      \"semi_synth_llm_noise_20pct_ec_hte_vs_global_rmse_ratio\": 2.8,\n500\t      \"semi_synth_llm_misspec_any_condition_ec_hte_benefit\": \"negligible\"\n501\t    },\n502\t    \"tables\": [\n503\t      {\n504\t        \"name\": \"Table: Robustness to Subgroup Label Noise (Synthetic Extreme, K=4, n=500)\",\n505\t        \"columns\": [\n506\t          \"noise_pct\",\n507\t          \"method\",\n508\t          \"mean_abs_bias\",\n509\t          \"rmse\",\n510\t          \"coverage_95\",\n511\t          \"rmse_ratio_vs_clean_ec_hte\"\n512\t        ],\n513\t        \"rows\": [\n514\t          [\n515\t            0,\n516\t            \"global\",\n517\t            0.105,\n518\t            0.142,\n519\t            0.94,\n520\t            \"-\"\n521\t          ],\n522\t          [\n523\t            0,\n524\t            \"ec_hte\",\n525\t            0.028,\n526\t            0.189,\n527\t            0.88,\n528\t            1.0\n529\t          ],\n530\t          [\n531\t            10,\n532\t            \"global\",\n533\t            0.105,\n534\t            0.144,\n535\t            0.94,\n536\t            \"-\"\n537\t          ],\n538\t          [\n539\t            10,\n540\t            \"ec_hte\",\n541\t            0.038,\n542\t            0.212,\n543\t            0.86,\n544\t            1.12\n545\t          ],\n546\t          [\n547\t            20,\n548\t            \"global\",\n549\t            0.105,\n550\t            0.147,\n551\t            0.93,\n552\t            \"-\"\n553\t          ],\n554\t          [\n555\t            20,\n556\t            \"ec_hte\",\n557\t            0.052,\n558\t            0.248,\n559\t            0.83,\n560\t            1.31\n561\t          ],\n562\t          [\n563\t            30,\n564\t            \"global\",\n565\t            0.105,\n566\t            0.15,\n567\t            0.93,\n568\t            \"-\"\n569\t          ],\n570\t          [\n571\t            30,\n572\t            \"ec_hte\",\n573\t            0.071,\n574\t            0.299,\n575\t            0.8,\n576\t            1.58\n577\t          ]\n578\t        ]\n579\t      },\n580\t      {\n581\t        \"name\": \"Table: Robustness to Misspecified K (Synthetic Extreme, n=500)\",\n582\t        \"columns\": [\n583\t          \"true_K\",\n584\t          \"model_K\",\n585\t          \"method\",\n586\t          \"mean_abs_bias\",\n587\t          \"rmse\",\n588\t          \"bias_reduction_vs_naive_pct\"\n589\t        ],\n590\t        \"rows\": [\n591\t          [\n592\t            4,\n593\t            4,\n594\t            \"ec_hte\",\n595\t            0.028,\n596\t            0.189,\n597\t            62\n598\t          ],\n599\t          [\n600\t            4,\n601\t            2,\n602\t            \"ec_hte\",\n603\t            0.048,\n604\t            0.265,\n605\t            35\n606\t          ],\n607\t          [\n608\t            \"continuous\",\n609\t            4,\n610\t            \"ec_hte\",\n611\t            0.041,\n612\t            0.227,\n613\t            45\n614\t          ],\n615\t          [\n616\t            4,\n617\t            4,\n618\t            \"global\",\n619\t            0.105,\n620\t            0.142,\n621\t            \"-\"\n622\t          ],\n623\t          [\n624\t            4,\n625\t            2,\n626\t            \"global\",\n627\t            0.105,\n628\t            0.142,\n629\t            \"-\"\n630\t          ]\n631\t        ]\n632\t      },\n633\t      {\n634\t        \"name\": \"Table: Robustness Under Worst-Tier Semi-Synthetic (Δπ<0.05, K=4, n=500)\",\n635\t        \"columns\": [\n636\t          \"condition\",\n637\t          \"method\",\n638\t          \"mean_abs_bias\",\n639\t          \"rmse\",\n640\t          \"rmse_ratio_vs_global\"\n641\t        ],\n642\t        \"rows\": [\n643\t          [\n644\t            \"clean\",\n645\t            \"global\",\n646\t            0.015,\n647\t            0.091,\n648\t            1.0\n649\t          ],\n650\t          [\n651\t            \"clean\",\n652\t            \"ec_hte\",\n653\t            0.012,\n654\t            0.182,\n655\t            2.0\n656\t          ],\n657\t          [\n658\t            \"noise_10pct\",\n659\t            \"ec_hte\",\n660\t            0.014,\n661\t            0.211,\n662\t            2.3\n663\t          ],\n664\t          [\n665\t            \"noise_20pct\",\n666\t            \"ec_hte\",\n667\t            0.016,\n668\t            0.255,\n669\t            2.8\n670\t          ],\n671\t          [\n672\t            \"misspec_k2\",\n673\t            \"ec_hte\",\n674\t            0.014,\n675\t            0.198,\n676\t            2.2\n677\t          ]\n678\t        ]\n679\t      }\n680\t    ],\n681\t    \"figures\": [],\n682\t    \"analysis\": \"Robustness analysis under worst-tier conditions serves a dual purpose: (1) On synthetic (mechanism active), EC-HTE degrades gracefully up to ~20% subgroup noise — HB partial pooling provides natural robustness by shrinking noisy subgroup estimates toward the global mean. This is a genuine positive finding. (2) On semi-synthetic with real LLM (mechanism dormant), misspecification compounds the existing EC-HTE disadvantage — RMSE ratio rises from 2.0x (clean) to 2.8x (20% noise). This is expected: when there is no bias to correct, adding noisy stratification structure only increases variance. For the paper reframe: present synthetic robustness as a positive property of the HB framework ('EC-HTE is robust to moderate misspecification when the mechanism is active'). The semi-synthetic results support the 'homogeneity boundary' argument — below the Δπ threshold, ANY stratification (correct or misspecified) is harmful. Include in appendix as robustness analysis, not main text. Key honest disclosure: 'EC-HTE requires both (a) the bias mechanism to be active (Δπ > threshold) and (b) reasonably accurate subgroup definitions (noise < ~20%) to provide RMSE improvement over global correction.'\\n\\nOn synthetic data (mechanism active), EC-HTE degrades gracefully under moderate subgroup mislabeling (10-20%) because HB partial pooling provides natural regularization — noisy subgroup boundaries cause the posterior to shrink toward the global mean, partially self-correcting. At 30% mislabeling, the subgroup signal-to-noise ratio collapses and EC-HTE RMSE becomes 1.58x the clean baseline, though bias is still lower than naive. When the true structure has K=4 but the model uses K=2, EC-HTE captures only the coarsest heterogeneity — bias reduction drops from ~60% (correct K) to ~35% vs naive, with a 1.4x RMSE penalty. Continuous-to-discrete misspecification is less harmful (1.2x RMSE) because the discretization still captures the dominant variation axis. CRITICAL: under worst-tier semi-synthetic conditions (Δπ < 0.05), ALL misspecification conditions make EC-HTE strictly worse than global on RMSE. When the mechanism is dormant, adding subgroup structure (correct or misspecified) only adds estimation noise with no bias reduction to compensate. The RMSE ratio rises to 2.8x at 20% noise (vs ~2.0x with clean subgroups), making EC-HTE even less attractive. This is the expected behavior: robustness analysis in the homogeneous regime simply confirms that unnecessary stratification hurts, and misspecification makes it hurt more.\\n\"\n683\t  }\n684\t]"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"161acbfe-115e-4501-915e-b96cb773dc21","uuid":"f2074858-f969-405e-b2af-a8dc3f278224","timestamp":"2026-05-21T04:03:25.543Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520/projects/ec_hte_llm_annotation/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T032758/worst/experiment_data.json","content":"[\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Real-data case study — UC Berkeley Measuring Hate Speech\",\n    \"description\": \"Apply EC-HTE to UC Berkeley hate speech corpus with demographic subgroups (race, gender, age, education). Annotate with GPT-4.1 via API. Compare global-corrected vs EC-HTE CATE estimates. Frame as 'Audit of Current Practice' — show that when LLM errors are homogeneous, global and EC-HTE converge, confirming theory predicts when correction is (un)necessary.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"llm_overall_accuracy\": 0.82,\n      \"llm_delta_pi_max_across_demographic_subgroups\": 0.03,\n      \"llm_delta_pi_race\": 0.02,\n      \"llm_delta_pi_gender\": 0.01,\n      \"llm_delta_pi_age\": 0.04,\n      \"llm_delta_pi_education\": 0.03,\n      \"global_vs_ec_hte_max_cate_shift_abs\": 0.018,\n      \"global_vs_ec_hte_max_cate_shift_se\": 0.35,\n      \"global_vs_ec_hte_mean_cate_shift_se\": 0.18,\n      \"num_subgroups_shift_exceeds_1se\": 0,\n      \"num_subgroups_shift_exceeds_0_5se\": 1,\n      \"ec_hte_vs_global_qualitative_agreement\": true,\n      \"bootstrap_ci_overlap_pct\": 100,\n      \"domain_knowledge_consistency\": \"both methods agree on direction of demographic effects\",\n      \"expert_labels_used\": 500,\n      \"n_total_llm_annotated\": 39565\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table: Real-Data CATE Estimates by Demographic Subgroup\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"subgroup\",\n          \"n\",\n          \"pi_hat_global\",\n          \"pi_hat_subgroup\",\n          \"cate_naive\",\n          \"cate_global\",\n          \"cate_ec_hte\",\n          \"shift_se\",\n          \"bootstrap_95ci_overlap\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"race_white\",\n            18200,\n            0.17,\n            0.16,\n            0.031,\n            0.028,\n            0.027,\n            0.15,\n            true\n          ],\n          [\n            \"race_nonwhite\",\n            21365,\n            0.17,\n            0.18,\n            0.044,\n            0.04,\n            0.041,\n            0.22,\n            true\n          ],\n          [\n            \"gender_male\",\n            20100,\n            0.17,\n            0.17,\n            0.039,\n            0.035,\n            0.035,\n            0.05,\n            true\n          ],\n          [\n            \"gender_female\",\n            19465,\n            0.17,\n            0.18,\n            0.036,\n            0.033,\n            0.034,\n            0.18,\n            true\n          ],\n          [\n            \"age_under30\",\n            15800,\n            0.17,\n            0.16,\n            0.042,\n            0.038,\n            0.037,\n            0.2,\n            true\n          ],\n          [\n            \"age_30plus\",\n            23765,\n            0.17,\n            0.19,\n            0.033,\n            0.03,\n            0.031,\n            0.28,\n            true\n          ],\n          [\n            \"edu_college\",\n            22100,\n            0.17,\n            0.17,\n            0.037,\n            0.034,\n            0.034,\n            0.08,\n            true\n          ],\n          [\n            \"edu_no_college\",\n            17465,\n            0.17,\n            0.2,\n            0.038,\n            0.034,\n            0.036,\n            0.35,\n            true\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table: Misclassification Heterogeneity Summary\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"demographic_dimension\",\n          \"n_subgroups\",\n          \"max_delta_pi\",\n          \"min_pi\",\n          \"max_pi\",\n          \"heterogeneity_regime\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"race\",\n            2,\n            0.02,\n            0.16,\n            0.18,\n            \"homogeneous\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"gender\",\n            2,\n            0.01,\n            0.17,\n            0.18,\n            \"homogeneous\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"age\",\n            2,\n            0.04,\n            0.16,\n            0.19,\n            \"homogeneous\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"education\",\n            2,\n            0.03,\n            0.17,\n            0.2,\n            \"homogeneous\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The real-data audit confirms the worst-tier prediction: on a well-studied English CSS task, SOTA LLMs produce near-homogeneous errors across demographic subgroups (max Δπ = 0.04 for age). Consequently, global and EC-HTE CATE estimates are statistically indistinguishable — the largest shift is 0.35 SE (education dimension), with 100% bootstrap CI overlap across all subgroups. This result has TWO interpretations for the paper: (1) Negative: EC-HTE provides no practical benefit on this dataset, undermining claims of immediate applicability. (2) Positive (reframed): The theory correctly predicts that correction is unnecessary when Δπ ≈ 0, validating the diagnostic framework. Frame as 'the bias identity correctly identifies the homogeneous regime.' Causal framework mapping: T = exposure to hate speech content, Y = perceived severity rating, Z = hate speech type (LLM-classified), S = demographic subgroup. Note limitation: causal interpretation is illustrative, not designed for rigorous causal inference on this observational corpus. CRITICAL PIVOT: use this result to motivate the 'Characterizing the Homogeneity Boundary' analysis — at what Δπ does the mechanism activate? Back-calculate from the bias identity: for bias > 0.05 (practical threshold), need Δπ > ~0.12 given observed Δτ. This characterization is itself a contribution — it tells practitioners when to worry.\\n\\n\\nGPT-4.1 achieves uniformly high accuracy on English hate speech classification — a well-studied CSS task with abundant training data. Demographic subgroups (race, gender, age, education of annotator or target) produce Δπ ≤ 0.04, well below the threshold where the bias mechanism becomes practically relevant. With near-homogeneous confusion matrices across subgroups, the global pooled CM is an adequate approximation of each subgroup CM, so global correction and EC-HTE produce statistically indistinguishable CATE estimates. All point estimate shifts fall within 0.35 SE — not actionable. This is exactly the prediction of the bias identity: when Δπ ≈ 0, bias ∝ Δπ×Δτ ≈ 0 regardless of CATE heterogeneity. The result is a negative finding for practical relevance but a positive finding for theory validation — the framework correctly identifies the homogeneous regime as safe for global correction.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Multiple LLM annotator comparison\",\n    \"description\": \"Annotate TweetEval sentiment with GPT-4.1 (API), Qwen3-235B-A22B (GPU), and Claude Sonnet 4.6 (API). Measure per-subgroup misclassification rates for each LLM. Compare EC-HTE benefit across LLMs of different quality tiers.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"gpt4_1_overall_accuracy\": 0.88,\n      \"gpt4_1_delta_pi_max\": 0.03,\n      \"gpt4_1_bias_amplification_median\": 1.08,\n      \"qwen3_overall_accuracy\": 0.81,\n      \"qwen3_delta_pi_max\": 0.05,\n      \"qwen3_bias_amplification_median\": 1.22,\n      \"claude_sonnet_overall_accuracy\": 0.86,\n      \"claude_sonnet_delta_pi_max\": 0.04,\n      \"claude_sonnet_bias_amplification_median\": 1.14,\n      \"ec_hte_rmse_ratio_vs_global_gpt4_1_n500_k4\": 2.1,\n      \"ec_hte_rmse_ratio_vs_global_qwen3_n500_k4\": 1.7,\n      \"ec_hte_rmse_ratio_vs_global_claude_n500_k4\": 1.9,\n      \"ec_hte_bias_reduction_pct_gpt4_1\": 8,\n      \"ec_hte_bias_reduction_pct_qwen3\": 22,\n      \"ec_hte_bias_reduction_pct_claude\": 14,\n      \"all_llms_homogeneous_regime\": true,\n      \"n_samples_annotated_per_llm\": 5000,\n      \"annotation_cost_gpt4_1_usd\": 12,\n      \"annotation_cost_claude_usd\": 18,\n      \"gpu_hours_qwen3\": 8\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table: LLM Comparison — Misclassification Heterogeneity\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"llm\",\n          \"accuracy\",\n          \"delta_pi_max\",\n          \"subgroup_with_max_delta\",\n          \"bias_amp_median\",\n          \"bias_amp_max\",\n          \"regime\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"GPT-4.1\",\n            0.88,\n            0.03,\n            \"has_hashtag\",\n            1.08,\n            1.15,\n            \"homogeneous\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Qwen3-235B\",\n            0.81,\n            0.05,\n            \"short_text\",\n            1.22,\n            1.29,\n            \"homogeneous\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Claude-Sonnet-4.6\",\n            0.86,\n            0.04,\n            \"has_hashtag\",\n            1.14,\n            1.21,\n            \"homogeneous\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table: EC-HTE vs Global by LLM (n_expert=500, K=4)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"llm\",\n          \"method\",\n          \"mean_abs_bias\",\n          \"rmse\",\n          \"coverage_95\",\n          \"bias_reduction_pct\",\n          \"rmse_ratio_vs_global\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"GPT-4.1\",\n            \"global\",\n            0.012,\n            0.089,\n            0.94,\n            \"-\",\n            1.0\n          ],\n          [\n            \"GPT-4.1\",\n            \"ec_hte\",\n            0.011,\n            0.187,\n            0.86,\n            8,\n            2.1\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Qwen3-235B\",\n            \"global\",\n            0.028,\n            0.095,\n            0.92,\n            \"-\",\n            1.0\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Qwen3-235B\",\n            \"ec_hte\",\n            0.022,\n            0.162,\n            0.84,\n            22,\n            1.7\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Claude-Sonnet-4.6\",\n            \"global\",\n            0.019,\n            0.091,\n            0.93,\n            \"-\",\n            1.0\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Claude-Sonnet-4.6\",\n            \"ec_hte\",\n            0.016,\n            0.173,\n            0.85,\n            14,\n            1.9\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The multi-LLM comparison reveals a compressed quality spectrum on TweetEval sentiment: even the weakest model (Qwen3, 0.81 accuracy) achieves Δπ_max = 0.05, barely above the homogeneity floor. This confirms the worst-tier hypothesis that standard English CSS tasks are 'solved enough' that misclassification heterogeneity is negligible. EC-HTE never dominates global on RMSE for any LLM. The reframing implication: move this table to appendix, use it to support the 'current SOTA is in the homogeneous regime' argument. The key forward-looking claim becomes: as researchers apply LLMs to harder tasks (cross-lingual, low-resource, domain-specialized coding — per Ashwin et al. 2025), Δπ will increase and the mechanism will activate. The multi-LLM comparison shows the mechanism is 'dormant' on easy tasks, not 'absent' — Qwen3's slightly higher Δπ hints at the direction of the gradient.\\n\\n\\nAll three SOTA LLMs achieve high accuracy on TweetEval sentiment (a 3-class task with well-defined categories: positive/negative/neutral). English sentiment is among the easiest CSS tasks — abundant training data, clear label semantics, minimal cultural ambiguity. GPT-4.1 leads (0.88 accuracy) with the smallest Δπ (0.03); Qwen3 is weakest (0.81) with slightly larger Δπ (0.05) because its English performance lags frontier models, but 0.05 is still in the homogeneous regime per the bias identity. Claude Sonnet falls between. All three LLMs produce Δπ < 0.05, confirming the worst-tier assumption that SOTA LLMs on standard English CSS tasks are 'too accurate and too uniform for heterogeneity to matter.' Bias amplification ranges 1.08–1.22x — within MC noise, not practically meaningful. EC-HTE has strictly worse RMSE than global for all three LLMs at n_expert ≤ 2000 because the per-subgroup CM estimation variance exceeds the (tiny) bias reduction. Even the weakest LLM (Qwen3) only shows 22% bias reduction with 1.7x RMSE penalty — a bad tradeoff. This result means the 'LLM quality spectrum' story partially collapses: the spectrum exists but is compressed into a narrow band where EC-HTE is never the right choice on this task.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Adaptive expert annotation allocation\",\n    \"description\": \"Implement CATE-weighted allocation targeting high-CATE × high-error subgroups. Compare against uniform and uncertainty-only allocation on synthetic data (where mechanism is active) and semi-synthetic with real LLM annotations (where mechanism is dormant).\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"synth_extreme_adaptive_vs_uniform_rmse_reduction_k2\": 0.18,\n      \"synth_extreme_adaptive_vs_uniform_rmse_reduction_k4\": 0.24,\n      \"synth_extreme_adaptive_vs_uncertainty_rmse_reduction_k4\": 0.09,\n      \"synth_extreme_optimal_allocation_ratio_high_error_sg\": 2.8,\n      \"semi_synth_llm_adaptive_vs_uniform_rmse_reduction_k4\": 0.02,\n      \"semi_synth_llm_adaptive_vs_uniform_bias_reduction\": 0.003,\n      \"semi_synth_llm_allocation_entropy\": 0.97,\n      \"convergence_iterations\": 3,\n      \"allocation_stability_across_seeds\": 0.91,\n      \"n_expert_total\": 500\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table: Allocation Strategy Comparison (K=4, n_expert=500)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"data_source\",\n          \"allocation\",\n          \"mean_abs_bias\",\n          \"rmse\",\n          \"bias_vs_uniform_pct\",\n          \"rmse_vs_uniform_pct\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"synthetic_extreme\",\n            \"uniform\",\n            0.062,\n            0.198,\n            0,\n            0\n          ],\n          [\n            \"synthetic_extreme\",\n            \"uncertainty_only\",\n            0.055,\n            0.178,\n            -11,\n            -10\n          ],\n          [\n            \"synthetic_extreme\",\n            \"cate_weighted\",\n            0.048,\n            0.151,\n            -23,\n            -24\n          ],\n          [\n            \"synthetic_moderate\",\n            \"uniform\",\n            0.031,\n            0.145,\n            0,\n            0\n          ],\n          [\n            \"synthetic_moderate\",\n            \"cate_weighted\",\n            0.027,\n            0.131,\n            -13,\n            -10\n          ],\n          [\n            \"semi_synth_llm\",\n            \"uniform\",\n            0.018,\n            0.142,\n            0,\n            0\n          ],\n          [\n            \"semi_synth_llm\",\n            \"cate_weighted\",\n            0.017,\n            0.139,\n            -6,\n            -2\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table: Allocation Weights by Subgroup (K=4, n_expert=500)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"data_source\",\n          \"subgroup\",\n          \"true_pi\",\n          \"true_cate\",\n          \"uniform_weight\",\n          \"adaptive_weight\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"synthetic_extreme\",\n            \"S0\",\n            0.05,\n            0.1,\n            0.25,\n            0.12\n          ],\n          [\n            \"synthetic_extreme\",\n            \"S1\",\n            0.1,\n            0.1,\n            0.25,\n            0.18\n          ],\n          [\n            \"synthetic_extreme\",\n            \"S2\",\n            0.2,\n            0.3,\n            0.25,\n            0.28\n          ],\n          [\n            \"synthetic_extreme\",\n            \"S3\",\n            0.3,\n            0.3,\n            0.25,\n            0.42\n          ],\n          [\n            \"semi_synth_llm\",\n            \"S0\",\n            0.12,\n            0.1,\n            0.25,\n            0.24\n          ],\n          [\n            \"semi_synth_llm\",\n            \"S1\",\n            0.13,\n            0.1,\n            0.25,\n            0.25\n          ],\n          [\n            \"semi_synth_llm\",\n            \"S2\",\n            0.14,\n            0.3,\n            0.25,\n            0.26\n          ],\n          [\n            \"semi_synth_llm\",\n            \"S3\",\n            0.15,\n            0.3,\n            0.25,\n            0.25\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Adaptive allocation is a Stage 3 / P2 experiment that, under worst-tier conditions, provides negligible empirical value with real LLM annotations. The fundamental issue: when Δπ is small, the signal that should guide allocation (error rate × CATE magnitude) is buried in estimation noise. The algorithm converges in 3 iterations but converges to near-uniform because it correctly identifies that no subgroup has substantially more to gain. For the paper reframe: mention adaptive allocation as a theoretical extension in the Discussion section ('when the mechanism activates, practitioners can further optimize expert budget allocation'). Do not include in main experimental results. Synthetic proof-of-concept in appendix is sufficient.\\n\\n\\nOn synthetic data where Δπ is large (extreme regime), adaptive allocation works as designed: directing more expert labels to high-error × high-CATE subgroups reduces RMSE by 18-24% over uniform allocation because those subgroups have the most to gain from better CM estimation. However, on semi-synthetic with real LLM annotations (worst-tier: Δπ < 0.05), adaptive allocation degenerates to near-uniform (allocation entropy 0.97 vs 1.0 for perfect uniform) because all subgroups have similarly low error rates and similarly uncertain CATEs. The CATE-weighting signal is dominated by estimation noise when true Δπ is small, so the algorithm cannot reliably identify which subgroups benefit most. RMSE improvement is 2% — within MC noise. This makes adaptive allocation a theoretically valid but practically irrelevant contribution under worst-tier conditions. Recommendation: present synthetic results in appendix as proof-of-concept; do not feature in main text.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Robustness to misspecified subgroup structure\",\n    \"description\": \"Test EC-HTE when subgroup definitions are noisy (random 10-30% mislabeling of subgroup membership), overlapping (soft subgroup assignment), or when the true DGP has a different subgroup structure than the conditioning variable used by EC-HTE.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"noise_10pct_ec_hte_rmse_ratio_vs_clean\": 1.12,\n      \"noise_20pct_ec_hte_rmse_ratio_vs_clean\": 1.31,\n      \"noise_30pct_ec_hte_rmse_ratio_vs_clean\": 1.58,\n      \"noise_10pct_ec_hte_still_beats_global_bias\": true,\n      \"noise_20pct_ec_hte_still_beats_global_bias\": true,\n      \"noise_30pct_ec_hte_worse_than_global_rmse\": true,\n      \"misspec_k2_for_k4_ec_hte_bias_reduction_vs_naive\": 0.35,\n      \"misspec_k2_for_k4_ec_hte_bias_reduction_vs_global\": 0.1,\n      \"misspec_k2_for_k4_rmse_penalty\": 1.4,\n      \"misspec_discrete_for_continuous_ec_hte_bias_reduction\": 0.45,\n      \"misspec_discrete_for_continuous_rmse_penalty\": 1.2,\n      \"hb_vs_mle_robustness_advantage_noise_20pct\": 0.08,\n      \"hb_shrinkage_mitigates_noise\": true,\n      \"semi_synth_llm_noise_20pct_ec_hte_vs_global_rmse_ratio\": 2.8,\n      \"semi_synth_llm_misspec_any_condition_ec_hte_benefit\": \"negligible\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table: Robustness to Subgroup Label Noise (Synthetic Extreme, K=4, n=500)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"noise_pct\",\n          \"method\",\n          \"mean_abs_bias\",\n          \"rmse\",\n          \"coverage_95\",\n          \"rmse_ratio_vs_clean_ec_hte\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            0,\n            \"global\",\n            0.105,\n            0.142,\n            0.94,\n            \"-\"\n          ],\n          [\n            0,\n            \"ec_hte\",\n            0.028,\n            0.189,\n            0.88,\n            1.0\n          ],\n          [\n            10,\n            \"global\",\n            0.105,\n            0.144,\n            0.94,\n            \"-\"\n          ],\n          [\n            10,\n            \"ec_hte\",\n            0.038,\n            0.212,\n            0.86,\n            1.12\n          ],\n          [\n            20,\n            \"global\",\n            0.105,\n            0.147,\n            0.93,\n            \"-\"\n          ],\n          [\n            20,\n            \"ec_hte\",\n            0.052,\n            0.248,\n            0.83,\n            1.31\n          ],\n          [\n            30,\n            \"global\",\n            0.105,\n            0.15,\n            0.93,\n            \"-\"\n          ],\n          [\n            30,\n            \"ec_hte\",\n            0.071,\n            0.299,\n            0.8,\n            1.58\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table: Robustness to Misspecified K (Synthetic Extreme, n=500)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"true_K\",\n          \"model_K\",\n          \"method\",\n          \"mean_abs_bias\",\n          \"rmse\",\n          \"bias_reduction_vs_naive_pct\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            4,\n            4,\n            \"ec_hte\",\n            0.028,\n            0.189,\n            62\n          ],\n          [\n            4,\n            2,\n            \"ec_hte\",\n            0.048,\n            0.265,\n            35\n          ],\n          [\n            \"continuous\",\n            4,\n            \"ec_hte\",\n            0.041,\n            0.227,\n            45\n          ],\n          [\n            4,\n            4,\n            \"global\",\n            0.105,\n            0.142,\n            \"-\"\n          ],\n          [\n            4,\n            2,\n            \"global\",\n            0.105,\n            0.142,\n            \"-\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table: Robustness Under Worst-Tier Semi-Synthetic (Δπ<0.05, K=4, n=500)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"condition\",\n          \"method\",\n          \"mean_abs_bias\",\n          \"rmse\",\n          \"rmse_ratio_vs_global\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"clean\",\n            \"global\",\n            0.015,\n            0.091,\n            1.0\n          ],\n          [\n            \"clean\",\n            \"ec_hte\",\n            0.012,\n            0.182,\n            2.0\n          ],\n          [\n            \"noise_10pct\",\n            \"ec_hte\",\n            0.014,\n            0.211,\n            2.3\n          ],\n          [\n            \"noise_20pct\",\n            \"ec_hte\",\n            0.016,\n            0.255,\n            2.8\n          ],\n          [\n            \"misspec_k2\",\n            \"ec_hte\",\n            0.014,\n            0.198,\n            2.2\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Robustness analysis under worst-tier conditions serves a dual purpose: (1) On synthetic (mechanism active), EC-HTE degrades gracefully up to ~20% subgroup noise — HB partial pooling provides natural robustness by shrinking noisy subgroup estimates toward the global mean. This is a genuine positive finding. (2) On semi-synthetic with real LLM (mechanism dormant), misspecification compounds the existing EC-HTE disadvantage — RMSE ratio rises from 2.0x (clean) to 2.8x (20% noise). This is expected: when there is no bias to correct, adding noisy stratification structure only increases variance. For the paper reframe: present synthetic robustness as a positive property of the HB framework ('EC-HTE is robust to moderate misspecification when the mechanism is active'). The semi-synthetic results support the 'homogeneity boundary' argument — below the Δπ threshold, ANY stratification (correct or misspecified) is harmful. Include in appendix as robustness analysis, not main text. Key honest disclosure: 'EC-HTE requires both (a) the bias mechanism to be active (Δπ > threshold) and (b) reasonably accurate subgroup definitions (noise < ~20%) to provide RMSE improvement over global correction.'\\n\\nOn synthetic data (mechanism active), EC-HTE degrades gracefully under moderate subgroup mislabeling (10-20%) because HB partial pooling provides natural regularization — noisy subgroup boundaries cause the posterior to shrink toward the global mean, partially self-correcting. At 30% mislabeling, the subgroup signal-to-noise ratio collapses and EC-HTE RMSE becomes 1.58x the clean baseline, though bias is still lower than naive. When the true structure has K=4 but the model uses K=2, EC-HTE captures only the coarsest heterogeneity — bias reduction drops from ~60% (correct K) to ~35% vs naive, with a 1.4x RMSE penalty. Continuous-to-discrete misspecification is less harmful (1.2x RMSE) because the discretization still captures the dominant variation axis. CRITICAL: under worst-tier semi-synthetic conditions (Δπ < 0.05), ALL misspecification conditions make EC-HTE strictly worse than global on RMSE. When the mechanism is dormant, adding subgroup structure (correct or misspecified) only adds estimation noise with no bias reduction to compensate. The RMSE ratio rises to 2.8x at 20% noise (vs ~2.0x with clean subgroups), making EC-HTE even less attractive. This is the expected behavior: robustness analysis in the homogeneous regime simply confirms that unnecessary stratification hurts, and misspecification makes it hurt more.\\n\"\n  }\n]","numLines":684,"startLine":1,"totalLines":684}}}
